The `Lookup` class and its associated changes introduce a new logical plan node for performing lookups in ESQL. The implementation seems mostly correct but there are some areas that need attention:
- In the `equals` method of `Lookup`, it incorrectly compares `tableName` with `matchFields`. It should compare `tableName` with `lookup.tableName`.
```java
@Override
public boolean equals(Object o) {
    if (this == o) {
        return true;
    }
    if (o == null || getClass() != o.getClass()) {
        return false;
    }
    if (super.equals(o) == false) {
        return false;
    }
    Lookup lookup = (Lookup) o;
    return Objects.equals(tableName, lookup.tableName)
        && Objects.equals(matchFields, lookup.matchFields)
        && Objects.equals(localRelation, lookup.localRelation);
}
```
- The `NodeInfo.create` method in the `Lookup` class should use the correct parameters. The current implementation uses `tableName` instead of `matchFields`.
```java
@Override
protected NodeInfo<? extends LogicalPlan> info() {
    return NodeInfo.create(this, Lookup::new, child(), tableName, matchFields);
}
```

In the `resolveLookup` method, when resolving attributes, it incorrectly resolves the attribute against the local output twice. It should resolve once and then check if the resolution was successful.
```java
else {
    ne = maybeResolveAttribute(ua, childrenOutput);
}
```
This line is unnecessary and can be removed.

The test case for `Lookup` should verify that the `tableName` and `matchFields` are correctly parsed.
```java
var plan = statement("ROW a = 1 | LOOKUP t ON j");
var lookup = as(plan, Lookup.class);
var tableName = as(lookup.tableName(), UnresolvedAttribute.class);
assertThat(tableName.name(), equalTo("t"));
assertThat(lookup.matchFields(), hasSize(1));
var matchField = as(lookup.matchFields().get(0), UnresolvedAttribute.class);
assertThat(matchField.name(), equalTo("j"));
```

In the `verify` method of `Verifier`, when checking for unresolved expressions in a `Lookup`, it should correctly handle the table name and match fields. The current implementation incorrectly checks the table name as if it were an attribute.
```java
var tableName = lookup.tableName();
if (tableName instanceof Unresolvable u) {
    failures.add(fail(tableName, u.unresolvedMessage()));
}
```

The test case for `testBasicLookup` should be implemented to verify the correct transformation of a `Lookup` into a `Join`.
```java
public void testBasicLookup() throws Exception {
    var plan = optimizedPlan("""
        from test
        | lookup int_number_names on int
        """);
    System.out.println(plan);
    // Add assertions to verify the plan structure
}
```

